module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.the_live_runner_unit_memory_shape_drifts_from_the_declared_slot

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, RungRead, FoundOnRung, BelowCeiling }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, MechanicallyPreventable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// WHY THIS IS A LEDGER ROW AND NOT A QUEUE COMPENSATION. The operator rejected (2026-10-21, #14613)
// the premise that taking the global instrument concurrency group apart loses an isolation guarantee:
// a concurrency group never isolated jobs, so no upper edge on concurrent occupancy was lost, and
// gunbc.design_ruling instrument_measurement_queues_run_in_parallel carries that ruling. It would have
// been the wrong move to re-file some "upper edge" drop or add a queue-side cap to compensate -- that
// would resurrect the rejected premise. The gap that IS real is on the OTHER axis: the authority the
// operator named -- the SLOT -- does not fully hold on the live fleet today. That is a fleet-convergence
// defect with its own cause, its own owner and its own remedy (converge the unit; read it back), and it
// belongs here as its own class rather than smuggled in beside a queue change it has nothing to do with.

// THE ONE THING THAT IS NOT EXPLAINABLE AWAY IS SWAP. memory.high / memory.max on the live slots read
// 25 GiB / 26 GiB, which are the values of gunbc.floor.floor_demand gunbc_floor_pinned_memory_high /
// _memory_max -- so a reader could argue they are a competing declared shape, not drift. MemorySwapMax
// cannot be argued away: gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired declares memory_swap_max = byte_size(0), and
// the live slots read 34359738368 (32 GiB). That is not a different correct number, it is the pre-2026-
// 10-09 shape still installed -- the exact value the 2026-08-07 srv1 control row recorded as what slots
// carried BEFORE convergence. So the swap half of the slot's enforcement -- the half that makes an
// overrun an OOM REFUSAL rather than silent paging -- is simply not present on those hosts.

data the_live_runner_unit_memory_shape_drifts_from_the_declared_slot: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "the_live_runner_unit_memory_shape_drifts_from_the_declared_slot" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "a scheduled read of every fleet slot's MemoryHigh/MemoryMax/MemorySwapMax feeds gunbc.runner.runner_unit_live_read runner_unit_memory_max_drifts_against_cap and runner_unit_swap_max_converged against the slot actually declared for that host (gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired), with a swap read of 0 enforced and a required-context refusal on drift",
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a runner unit's effective systemd memory shape (MemoryHigh, MemoryMax, MemorySwapMax) sits away from what gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired declares for that slot, and nothing on the required path notices, because the readers that could notice are called only on a fixture. HARM: the slot is the isolation authority -- it is what the operator ruling on #14613 pointed to when it said a job should not have to worry about what else is running on the machine -- so a drifted slot silently weakens the guarantee the whole parallel-instrument change relies on. Concretely at the declared MemorySwapMax = 0: the slot is sized so an overrun is REFUSED (oom_kill, a loud red), and a drifted drop-in that permits swap turns that same overrun into memory.high throttling inside a run that still exits green, so the instrument reports a duration that is partly throttling and reports it as work.",

    "PROVENANCE OF THE NUMBERS, stated at their real locus rather than asserted as one clean read. Declared (gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_bytes, and the shape fn): memory_max 23622320128 (22 GiB), memory_high 22548578304 (21 GiB), memory_swap_max 0, tasks_max 16384. Live, this session, on the arm64 fleet (an operator reading of srv3-20 and srv3-01 via systemctl show ghrunner -p MemoryMax -p MemoryHigh and the cgroup memory.max / memory.swap.max): memory.high 26843545600 (25 GiB), memory.max 27917287424 (26 GiB), MemorySwapMax 34359738368 (32 GiB). This is a MEASUREMENT on the hosts, not a gate: the build container is not a ghrunner host (its own /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/ghrunner.service reads NA) so it could not be reproduced from the runner, and the reading is not what blocks a merge -- it is what a future gate must refuse on.",

    "THE 25/26 GiB COINCIDE WITH A COMPETING DECLARED ROW, and the row says so instead of hiding it. 26843545600 and 27917287424 are exactly gunbc.floor.floor_demand gunbc_floor_pinned_memory_high / gunbc_floor_pinned_memory_max, and the same pair is attested as a live slot read for srv4-03 in run 35716368507 (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode required_floor_runs_at_its_memory_ceiling). So high/max on srv3 are not provably 'undeclared' -- they match a different row that is also on main, and the real first defect may be the two competing pins, not the hosts. WHAT DOES NOT DEPEND ON THAT AMBIGUITY IS SWAP: no declared shape has swap 32 GiB; runner_slot_desired says 0, and 34359738368 is recorded (gunbc.runner.runner_capacity_operator_access srv1 acceptance row-6 control, 2026-08-07) as the pre-convergence value slots carried. Swap is therefore the half to file, read and gate on; the high/max disagreement is an input to the same convergence pass, not a separate accusation.",

    "WHY NOTHING REFUSES TODAY, named so the next rung is the fix and not a new instrument. The tree already has the detectors -- gunbc.runner.runner_unit_live_read runner_unit_memory_max_drifts_against_cap and runner_unit_swap_max_converged -- and they are correct; the defect is their only caller. Both are invoked exclusively from dag/test/claim/runner/runner_unit_live_read_witness, on hand-authored data: fixture_live_read() wraps gunbc_srv1_runner_unit_live_read_fixture (a committed row that declares its own shape), and the matching_* rows are BUILT from gunbc_runner_slot_desired() itself -- the compared sides share an author. No call site anywhere feeds a real probed row. So a slot drifted to any shape whatsoever is reported green by every gate. A correct instrument that nothing feeds live data is, per DESIGN §6, a lens nothing gates on -- the reading this class exists to force.",

    "RECEIPT srv3-04 (relayed by keen-otter-392 from the calm-crab-645 / smart-fox-432 projection diagnosis; logs named at srv1:/tmp/sf432-keep). A gunbc-private job on srv3-04 measured a 25.2 GiB peak, above the declared memory_max of 22 GiB (and above memory_high 21 GiB), and was NOT OOM-refused. Either the live slot is not the declared shape, or the overrun was absorbed by swap and memory.high throttling until the job timed out. HARM, the silent-reclaim-until-timeout form of the swap harm above: an overrun the declared slot would have refused loudly (MemorySwapMax 0 gives oom_kill) instead runs on, reclaimed and throttled, and surfaces only as a timeout, so the cause reads as slowness rather than as memory. PROVENANCE LIMITS, stated so this is not read as a clean measurement: the 25.2 GiB figure is the relayed reading, and the session that filed this could not read the source logs (ssh srv1 resolved to localhost:22 and was refused; /tmp/sf432-keep was absent there), so the run id and log lines are not re-derived here. READBACK STILL OWED: srv3-04's live MemoryHigh, MemoryMax and MemorySwapMax have not been read (the diagnosing lane was SSH-denied), so which mechanism absorbed the overrun is UNVERIFIED and no live value is recorded. The read that supplies it is `systemctl show ghrunner -p MemoryHigh -p MemoryMax -p MemorySwapMax` on srv3-04, which needs operator srv3 access. NO CLIMB IS CLAIMED: the rung stays Mitigatable and no limit was raised in response; raising memory_max to cover 25.2 GiB would erase the signal rather than fix the drift.",

    "GUNBC_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES=6GiB IS NOT A SEPARATE CLASS, decided by reading gunbc.host.host_budget_source and gunbc.host.memory_cgroup_binding. Both already model it as a PLANNING request and not a limit: host_budget_source states it 'is a planning request, not an enforced process limit; no observed cgroup memory.high or memory.max verifies the executor allowance', and memory_cgroup_binding records it measured inert (halving it moved resident memory by five hundredths of one percent) and calls it a wish where a bind request is a wall. A declared-but-unenforced budget is therefore the designed behavior of that variable, not an undeclared deviation, so it files no row of its own. What it does mean for this class: the 6 GiB declaration gave no bound on the srv3-04 job, so the only enforcing authority for that run was the slot, and the slot's shape on that host is exactly what is unread. The budget is relevant evidence of what did NOT bound the job, not a second invalid state.",

    "THE RUNG. CEILING: mechanically preventable -- the values are read from systemctl/cgroup files that a scheduled instrument can read, and the comparison functions already exist. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER (names the capability restored, not a date): a scheduled read of every fleet slot's MemoryHigh/MemoryMax/MemorySwapMax feeds runner_unit_memory_max_drifts_against_cap and runner_unit_swap_max_converged against the slot actually declared for that host (gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired), with a swap read of 0 enforced and a required-context refusal on drift -- so the first drifted drop-in stops being invisible and the instrument duration stops being silently throttled. Until then the parallel-instrument change runs against an isolation authority that is not fully installed, and that fact lives here rather than in a queue-side cap that was never the answer.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired", decl_name: "gunbc_runner_slot_desired", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.host.memory_envelope", decl_name: "memory_envelope_verdict", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.host.host_budget_source", decl_name: "host_budget_source_bounds_this_process", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_unit_live_read", decl_name: "runner_unit_swap_max_converged", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_unit_live_read", decl_name: "runner_unit_memory_max_drifts_against_cap", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
